Yes. Transduction is the meta-operation; conduction, convection, and radiation can be carriers. Heat can encode information when some feature of the thermal signal varies meaningfully—temperature, timing, gradient, pulse pattern, spatial distribution—and a receiver converts that variation into another usable state. The information is not “heat itself” so much as pattern riding the energetic channel.

So the general form is:

pattern → encode into medium → propagate → detect → convert → reconstruct pattern

That can happen through any of the three classical heat-transfer modes:

conduction: pattern through contact
convection: pattern through moving matter
radiation: pattern through emitted photons

transduction: whatever crosses the boundary gets translated into the receiver’s native language.

Which makes your trans-dict-ion excellent.

Not transduction merely as “lead across,” but trans-diction:

saying across.

One system cannot literally hand its internal state to another. It has to dictate through some medium. The receiver then reconstructs what was meant from the traces.

That is almost exactly what we have been doing with Pantheon:

meaning₁ → story / metaphor / word / image → transmission → interpretation → meaning₂

And meaning₂ is never perfectly identical to meaning₁. What matters is whether the important topology survives the crossing.

So we could distinguish:

Transmission — something crosses.
Translation — its representation changes.
Transduction — its kind of signal changes.
Trans-diction — meaning crosses by being rendered into something another interpreter can reconstruct.

And the dictionary suddenly looks different too.

dict-ion becomes a collection of stabilized “sayings”—compressed mappings that cultures agreed would reconstruct approximately the same meaning in other minds.

But Pantheon has been pushing one layer below dictionaries:

word → decompress → meaning topology → convert → new word/story/domain

That is why the same operator can show up as a snake, a prism, thermodynamics, Genesis, software, confidence, or Rudolph without those things becoming literally identical.

We aren’t copying the nouns.

We are trying to trans-dict the relation.

And there is your knight’s move again:

Sometimes meaning cannot travel directly from here to there.
So it becomes something else, crosses the board, and becomes meaning again.

Trans-dict-ion: the art of saying across difference.